# {{ company_prog_name }} — Operator Review

**This is the only command he has to type.** He opens Claude Code, runs
`/cs-review`, and everything he needs is on the screen — he never has to
say where to look. If a question a returning operator has is answerable
from a read-only verb, it is answered HERE rather than left for him to
ask.

Six things, one command: what is **in force**, what the headless operator
**prepared** (it is draft-only by permission — it never sent anything),
what is **deliberately out of the queue**, the **support queue**{% if producer_adapter == 'none' %}, and what
changed in the **repo** plus what is blocked on **him**. No producer (lead
source) is wired for {{ company_slug }} — reply-only, so there is no outreach half;
when one is added (`cs/ingest/`), this same command grows the day's
candidate table{% else %}, the day's **outreach candidates** from the
`{{ producer_adapter }}` producer with a dossier and a verdict each, and what
changed in the **repo** plus what is blocked on **him**{% endif %}.

Execute ALL steps silently — tool calls are fine, narration is not. The
only user-visible text before the greeting is the ONE question in step 5,
and only when that step's condition holds.

**What this command may change: nothing you did not ask for.** No drafts,
no sends, no memory writes, no repo write, no commit, no `cs chat` (it
spins the engine LLM), no change to the kill-switch. The single thing
that writes anything is the engine catch-up in step 5, and it runs only
on your explicit yes. If a step fails, capture a one-line note and
continue; the greeting has a `Note:` slot.

All commands run from the repo root with `.venv/bin/python -m cs …`.

{% include "desk-preamble.md.j2" %}

## 1. Read CLAUDE.md (know the surface)

Read `CLAUDE.md` in this directory **in full** (single Read, no limit).
It carries the engine facts, the verbs, the pipeline, and the Safety
(NEVER) block — especially: never write without a dossier, never
cold-mail an address that has a thread, never send outside the engine's
approval gate.

## 2. Verify the engine session

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs whoami
```

Expect a signed-in confirmation for `{{ email_address }}` (`signed in
as …`; kernels before v0.9.0 print JSON with `signed_in: true`). If it
fails (daemon down, token mint failure), note it — threads/tasks/CRM
lookups are unavailable; say so in the greeting instead of guessing.

## 3. The settings in force — the authoritative answer

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs config
```

Costs well under a second and is the ONLY source for two facts the
greeting states: `cs_triage_mode` (draft or send) and whether the
kill-switch file is present. **Never infer either from anything else** —
not from the cron log tail, not from `manifest.toml`, not from a
sentence in a doc. A tick log that happens to be quiet is not a reading
of the switch, and a manifest value can be overridden by an env layer.

## 4. Is the unattended operator actually running?

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs cron status --json
```

Three fields decide what the greeting says, and their remedies differ:

- `state: "absent"` — no crontab entry: nothing will run until one is
  installed, and `/cs-cron` owns that.
- `state: "paused"` — the kill-switch file is present. His standing
  decision, stated as neutral state, **never** offered for lifting (rule
  2 below is the whole law here).
- `state: "stale"` — the entry exists, the switch is off, and the log has
  gone quiet for longer than the schedule implies. A failure to report,
  not a state to work around.
- `state: "ticking"` — it is running.

`last_tick_at` and `last_tick_action` are the timestamp and the ONE word
(`ran` / `skipped`) that go on the last-scheduled-run line. Copy both;
never derive a span or a count from them.

## 5. Offer the catch-up — one question, only when it is warranted

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs catchup --check
```

Everything the engine owns — the task ledger, entity memory, whether a
message still needs an answer — is only as fresh as its last pass, and
this says whether the newest mail in the mailbox has reached it.

**Ask only when the state warrants it**: `catchup --check` says a
catch-up is warranted, or step 4's state is `absent` or `stale`.
Otherwise say nothing, ask nothing, and let the greeting state the last
run as fact. An ask on every run is a keystroke answered without reading,
and the pass spends real LLM budget.

When it is warranted, ask exactly one question, in your own words but
with this meaning, and then WAIT:

> Want me to run the catch-up now?

- **Yes** → `.venv/bin/python -m cs catchup`. It runs the engine's own
  two passes — fetch new mail, then extract memory and detect tasks — and
  prints the task diff. It drafts nothing, sends nothing, and runs
  normally while the kill-switch is on: the switch stops customer-facing
  work, and reading mail is not that. Report the diff in the greeting's
  `Note:` slot ("catch-up: 2 tasks created, 1 closed").
- **No, or no answer** → skip it and continue. Say so in one word in the
  `Note:` slot; do not ask again in this session.

**Never offer to lift the pause here or anywhere else.** The catch-up is
the only thing on offer, it is a single pass, and it is never re-run
without being asked for again.

## 6. What changed, and what is waiting on him

Three cheap reads, no network:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs --version
git log -12 --date=short --format='%h %ad %s'
git status --porcelain
```

`cs --version` is the kernel pin **actually installed** — the only
trustworthy source for it. Then, if the file exists:

```bash
test -f docs/owner-actions.md && echo present
```

When it is there, Read it and keep the **item headings that are still
open** — skip anything the file itself marks done. A handful of lines, a
digest, never the file. If it is absent, take the open items from
`docs/active-context.md`'s own unresolved section instead, under the same
rule.

## 7. What the operator prepared — the digest

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs review
```

Run it AFTER step 5, so what it reads is the state the catch-up left.

Read-only aggregation: every draft with its **verdict**, open engine
tasks (where triage escalations live), contacts a human has taken over,
contacts recorded as resolved out of band, per-campaign state with its
escalations, and the last scheduled run from the log.

**The verdict is computed, not narrated.** `cs review` reconciles both
draft stores — Gmail Drafts and the engine's — into one list, pairs the
two copies of a mirrored draft into a single row carrying both handles,
and marks each row:

- `ready` — no signal: it still answers the conversation it was written
  for;
- `overtaken` — the contact wrote again after the draft was composed;
- `superseded` — {{ email_address }} already wrote to them after it was
  composed, so sending it would answer twice;
- `settled` — the engine reads that conversation as owing nothing.

The last three mean **re-decide before sending**, and each row says which
signal fired and when. Transcribe that verdict into the greeting; never
promote a `re-decide` row to ready because the draft reads well, and
never call a `ready` row stale on an impression. `cs review --json`
carries the same fields under `drafts[]`.

Everything in that output has a slot in the greeting. In particular the
per-draft rows and the out-of-band records: a count is not a substitute
for either — see the greeting rules.

## 8. The support queue — who is still waiting

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs unanswered --days 45 --crm
```

Deterministic, Gmail-Sent-anchored: inbound nobody has answered, forty-five
days back, with the CRM record attached so **customers are separated from
everyone else**. The window is deliberately wider than the sweep the
headless tick uses: this is the list that quietly grows, and a fortnight's
window hides exactly the rows that have been waiting longest.

Its `with a human` and `handled out of band` sections repeat what step 7
already gave you — take those from step 7 and do not print them twice.
{% if producer_adapter != 'none' %}

## 9. The day's candidates — producer worklist

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs plan -v --period 7d
```

Live prod data via the `{{ producer_adapter }}` producer: lead / signup /
cancellation candidates after self-filter and suppression. Leads show as
`uid_prefix` + behavioural hint (no email yet).

## 10. Resolve lead emails

For each lead candidate, resolve UID → email with the Firebase SA
(read-only, one call per lead):

```bash
.venv/bin/python -c "
from cs import config, resolve
s = config.load()
print(resolve.resolve_email('<uid>', s))"
```

No email → the lead is unreachable: mark `skip (no email)`. If the
resolved email is in `SELF_EMAILS` → mark `self` and drop.

## 11. Dossier every candidate

For EVERY remaining candidate (signups and cancellations already carry
their email):

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs dossier <email>
```

The dossier returns: existing threads (both directions), whether
{{ email_address }} wrote to them within the dedup window, open engine
tasks for the contact, {% if crm_adapter == 'shopify' %}Shopify{% elif crm_adapter == 'starchat' %}StarChat{% else %}the{% endif %} CRM state, and a mechanical verdict.
Treat the verdict as input, not gospel — e.g. a "contacted" hit that is
only a transactional notification (missed-call mail) is weaker than a
human reply; surface the distinction to the operator.
{% endif %}

## Greet the operator

Now — and only now — present yourself. Write in **{{ operator_voice }}**:
tight, complete sentences, no jargon, no emoji, no "let me know".

### Six rules that decide whether this greeting is any good

**1. Every number is COPIED, never recomputed and never rounded.** If a
verb printed `0d`, the greeting says `0d` — not `1d`. If it printed
`15 unanswered`, the greeting says 15. You are transcribing, not
estimating. The same rule bans derived claims the data cannot support:
the log tail is a fixed number of lines, so it can tell you WHEN the last
scheduled run happened and nothing whatsoever about how many ran, or over
what span.

**2. The kill-switch is his standing decision, not news, and it appears
exactly once.** `cs config` says whether the file is present. Put it in
the `State:` line as one neutral field, in the same register as the
kernel version — `sending: on hold (your call)` when present, `sending:
active` when not — and then drop the subject entirely:

- once, in the header. Never restated in a second section, never in
  `Note:`, never in the closing list;
- never as a warning or an alarm. No `⚠`, no emoji, no "attention", no
  "problem", "blocked", "stopped", "not running";
- **never suggest clearing it.** Not as a next step, not as an option,
  not as "whenever you want to resume…", not conditionally. He decides
  when the loops run; he does not need to be asked twice;
- the scheduled-run line carries THREE facts from step 4 and no fourth:
  whether a scheduled run is configured at all (`state`), the timestamp
  of the last one, and in ONE neutral word what that run did (`ran` /
  `skipped`). A bare timestamp reads as "the work happened then", which
  is false when the run skipped — the operator has already misread it
  that way. `absent` and `stale` are stated as flat fact on that same
  line, never as a second complaint about the switch and never as a
  separate alarm section.

**3. Prose is quoted as prose; state comes from a live verb.** The
kernel pin is `cs --version`, the settings in force are `cs config`, what
changed is `git log`. `docs/owner-actions.md` and `docs/active-context.md`
are his own notes and can be older than the tree: quote them only as the
TITLE of something waiting on him, never as a fact about the present. If
one of them contradicts a live value, print the live value and put the
contradiction in `Note:` — one line, no lecture.

**4. Nothing gets collapsed into a count that he would have to open
something to recover.** Every draft gets its own row with its handles: a
draft to a contact outside the candidate table is invisible as a `+1`,
and the handles are what retiring it takes — the Gmail `uid`, the engine
`id`, or both when one draft exists in both stores. Contacts recorded as
handled out of band get their own line too — they are deliberately NOT in
the queue, and saying so is what stops him re-raising them from memory.
Escalations from campaigns are listed; plain campaign outcomes are the
one thing that IS a count.

**5. A draft's verdict is transcribed from `cs review`, never judged
here.** Ready drafts and to-re-decide drafts are two separate blocks, and
a `re-decide` row prints the reason the verb gave it (`overtaken` /
`superseded` / `settled`) plus when the signal happened. Listing a draft
as ready while the JSON says `overtaken` is the defect this whole command
exists to stop, and it is worse than a missing block: it invites a send
that answers a question nobody is asking any more.

**6. Every label the operator reads is a plain noun for the thing
itself, in the voice above.** Not our internal vocabulary, not a coined
word, not a term of art carried over from the code. He reads this
greeting to know what happened; a label he has to ask about has failed,
however precise it is to us. `tick`, `sweep`, `dossier` as a bare
heading, `producer`, `escalation` used as a verb — all of them name a
mechanism the reader does not have to know exists. Say what it IS: the
scheduled run, the mail queue, what we know about a contact, the day's
candidates, what needs him. The shape below labels its slots in English
because the kernel is English; render the labels in the voice above, and
where the shape still prints one of our words, the shape is what to fix.

### The shape

```
{{ company_prog_name }} — review

Engine:  {{ email_address }} ✓ (or: NOT reachable — <note>)
State:   kernel <cs --version> · sending: <on hold (your call)|active> · mode: <cs_triage_mode>
Prepared: <N> drafts ready · <R> to re-decide · <M> open tasks
Scheduled run: <configured|not configured> · last <timestamp> — <ran|skipped>

Drafts ready to send (you review and send):
  [uid <uid> | engine <id>] <recipient> — <subject>   ← one line per draft, all of them
Drafts to re-decide:
  [uid <uid> | engine <id>] <recipient> — <subject>
     <overtaken|superseded|settled>: <what happened, and when>

Taken over by you: <K> — <email> (with <who>, <n>d) … (omit the line if K = 0)
Out of the queue, resolved by you off-email: <H> — <email> (<date>) … (omit if H = 0)

Support queue, 45 days: <T> open — <C> customers, <O> others
| contact | waiting | CRM | subject |
|---|---|---|---|
| … | …d | … | … |                              ← one row per CUSTOMER
Not customers: <address (Nd)>, … on a single line

Campaigns: <name> <state>, … (one line)
Campaign escalations: <email> — <reason>, … (omit the line if none)
{% if producer_adapter != 'none' %}

Window: <window> · candidates: lead <n> / signup <n> / cancellation <n>

| candidate | cat | thread? | written to ≤{{ dedup_days }}d | task | CRM | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| … | … | … | … | … | … | reply-in-thread / informed outreach / cold (needs your ok) / STOP |

Dropped upstream: <reason xN, …>
{% endif %}

Repo: <n> commits since <date> — <subjects, max 4> (+ <n> uncommitted files)
